+  // What a `--proxy-server` value carries once its userinfo is taken off it.
+  struct ProxyServerValue {
+    // The value with every `user:pass@` span removed: the string Chromium's
+    // proxy URI parser is handed. Byte-identical to the input when the input
+    // carried no userinfo, so a launch without credentials rewrites nothing.
+    std::string endpoint;
+
+    // The one credential the value carried, if it carried one.
+    std::optional<ProxyCredentials> credentials;
+
+    // Non-empty when the value must refuse the launch, and is the reason.
+    // Returned rather than acted on so that the parse is a pure function the
+    // unit tests can drive; the caller is the one that refuses.
+    std::string refusal;
+  };
+
+  // Separates `--proxy-server`'s credential from its endpoint.
+  //
+  // `scheme://user:pass@host:port` is the syntax every other proxy tool on
+  // earth accepts, and Chromium's proxy URI parser has no userinfo concept at
+  // all: `ProxyUriToProxyServer` hands everything after `://` to
+  // `ParseHostAndPort`, which fails on the `@`, the chain becomes unparseable
+  // and every request ends `ERR_NO_SUPPORTED_PROXIES`. So the credential is
+  // taken off here, at the boundary, and Chromium is handed the endpoint
+  // alone.
+  //
+  // The value is not one URL. `ProxyConfig::ProxyRules::ParseFromString`
+  // reads it as `;`-separated entries, each either a bare proxy-URI list or
+  // `url_scheme=<list>`, each list `,`-separated. Userinfo is taken off every
+  // URI in it, or `http=http://u:p@a:8080;socks5://u:p@b:1080` would leave a
+  // secret in argv and still fail to parse.
+  //
+  // Splitting follows the URL Standard, which is what the Node launcher's
+  // `new URL()` applies to the same field, so one proxy URL yields one
+  // credential whichever entry point receives it: the **last** `@` delimits
+  // the userinfo, so a password containing `@` needs no escaping, and the
+  // **first** `:` inside it delimits the password, so one containing `:`
+  // needs none either. Both halves are percent-decoded per RFC 3986 §2.1.
+  //
+  // Refuses, rather than guessing, when: two URIs in one value carry
+  // different credentials, since the store holds one; the userinfo holds a
+  // malformed escape, an embedded NUL or non-UTF-8 bytes, all of which the
+  // launcher's `decodeURIComponent` also rejects; either half exceeds the
+  // 4096-byte bound the envelope loader enforces; or the scheme cannot
+  // authenticate at all (`direct://`, `quic://`, anything unknown), where a
+  // credential is a typo and silently dropping it would authenticate nothing
+  // while reporting success.
+  static ProxyServerValue ParseProxyServerValue(std::string_view value);
+
+  // `encoded` without its `proxy_credentials` block, and that block in
+  // `credentials` when it is well-formed.
+  //
+  // Returns nullopt when the payload carries no such block or cannot be
+  // decoded, meaning the caller leaves the switch exactly as it found it. A
+  // block that is present but malformed is still removed, and `credentials`
+  // stays empty with the same line logged that the loader logs, so an
+  // oversized credential is ignored exactly as it always was and no secret is
+  // left behind on the command line either way.
+  static std::optional<std::string> PayloadWithoutCredentials(
+      const std::string& encoded,
+      std::optional<ProxyCredentials>* credentials);
+
+  // `encoded` carrying `credentials`, for a child's command line.
+  //
+  // An empty `encoded` yields `{"device_profile":{},"proxy_credentials":{…}}`,
+  // the shape the launcher sends for a launch that authenticates to a proxy
+  // and authors no device. Otherwise the block is set beside whatever the
+  // payload already is -- the envelope form or a bare profile -- because
+  // ParseOrNull reads it before it unwraps an envelope, so both forms carry
+  // it. Returns nullopt when the payload cannot be decoded or the result
+  // cannot be serialised.
+  static std::optional<std::string> PayloadWithCredentials(
+      const std::string& encoded,
+      const ProxyCredentials& credentials);
+
+  // Takes this launch's proxy credentials off this process's command line and
+  // holds them in memory for the rest of it.
+  //
+  // Two sources, one store: `--proxy-server` userinfo and an
+  // `--apostate-profile` envelope's `proxy_credentials` block. Supplying both
+  // refuses the launch -- they are two sources of truth for one store, and
+  // picking one silently is how an operator ends up authenticating with the
+  // credential they did not mean.
+  //
+  // Nothing is left on the command line, and that is the point rather than
+  // tidiness. `chrome://version`, `chrome://gpu`, a net-export capture's
+  // `clientInfo.command_line`, DevTools `SystemInfo.getInfo` and the
+  // crash-key switch list all serialise this process's command line verbatim.
+  // A credential that is not on it cannot reach any of them, which closes
+  // those five paths by construction rather than by redacting each one and
+  // hoping the next is remembered.
+  //
+  // Browser process only, and it must run before the first Get() and before
+  // any child command line is built. `PreSandboxStartup` in
+  // chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc is that point for the same reasons
+  // patch 0084 put the compositor there, and it runs immediately ahead of it
+  // so the compositor sees a payload with no credential in it.
+  static void AdoptLaunchProxyCredentials();
+
+  // Puts the adopted credentials onto a child's command line, inside the
+  // `--apostate-profile` envelope.
+  //
+  // That envelope is the only channel a child has: `--proxy-server` is not
+  // copied to children, and the network stack that consumes the credentials
+  // -- `connect_job_params_factory.cc`, `SOCKS5DatagramClientSocket`, the
+  // WebRTC socket factory -- runs in the network service. A no-op when
+  // nothing was adopted, so a launch without credentials builds a child
+  // command line byte-identical to before.
+  static void AppendLaunchProxyCredentials(CommandLine* command_line);
+
